 build` for the SvelteKit assets, then\nhands off to `tauri-action`, which builds the bundle, attaches it to the Release, and —\nwhen the updater is configured (ADR 0018) — writes the signed `latest.json` manifest."],[782,"**1. Default port → 22345**, for both hosts. Desktop binds `127.0.0.1:22345`; the server's\n`--bind` default becomes `0.0.0.0:22345`."],[725,"**1. New crate `crates/shalgalt-store`.** It depends on `shalgalt-core` (for the trait +\nDTOs) and `rusqlite` (`bundled`), and exports `SqliteStore` implementing `DataStore`, plus\na `DeferredReadOnlyStore` for the desktop. Workspace §8.1/§8.2 is updated to list it."],[930,"**1. The updater is opt-in and OFF by default.** `tauri-plugin-updater&#x60; is wired in, but\nno update check fires on launch. A **\"Check for updates\" toggle** lives in app\npreferences, next to the theme / comfortable-typography controls (ADR 0006). The plugin\nonly contacts the network after a teacher turns it on (or presses an explicit\n\"Check now\"). On a fresh install and on every offline machine, the app makes **zero**\nupdate-related network calls — satisfying §6.10 and the offline-school requirement."],[685,"**1. Version under `/v1/`.** All resource routes are `/v1/{exams,templates,results}`.\n`/healthz` and `/openapi.json` sit outside the version prefix (they are service metadata,\nnot part of the data contract). A breaking change to any `/v1/` shape requires a `/v2/`\nand a new ADR."],[144,"**1.5 mm**"],[28,"**2–3 min**"],[686,"**2. A `DataStore` trait is the persistence seam.** Core defines `DataStore` (object-safe,\n`Send + Sync`) plus the DTOs that cross it (`ExamDto`, `TemplateDto`, `ResultDto`, the\n`New*` request bodies). `AppState` holds an `Arc<dyn DataStore>`; every handler reads/writes\nthrough it. Core ships an in-memory `MemoryStore` for tests. Each host injects a concrete\nimplementation (see ADR 0014). Core still touches no database."],[783,"**2. Desktop auto-fallback.** `api::spawn` resolves a *desired* port (from\n`SHALGALT_API_PORT`, else 22345) and binds the first free port in `[desired, desired+16)`.\nOnly `AddrInUse` triggers fallback; other bind errors return immediately so a real\nmisconfiguration is not masked. The chosen port rides along in `ApiHandle::port()`."],[877,"**2. OS rollout follows master-plan priority.** Windows (NSIS + MSI) is the first and\nmandatory target. macOS (`.dmg`) and Linux (AppImage + `.deb`) jobs exist in the same\nworkflow but may be left cert-blocked / `continue-on-error` until P9 per master plan\n§6.9; the *workflow shape* is built once so enabling an OS later is a flag flip, not a\nrewrite."],[931,"**2. The update manifest is a static `latest.json` on `gh-pages`.** The canonical endpoint\nis:"],[726,"**2. Two postures.**"],[136,"**2.5 mm**"],[1406,"**200–300 DPI** хангалттай. Хэт өндөр DPI файлыг л томруулна, чанар нэмэгдэхгүй."],[687,"**3. DTOs are separate from `domain::*`.** The wire shapes are their own types so the REST\ncontract can evolve without dragging the grading/IPC models. Opaque payloads\n(`templates.json_schema`, `results.detail_answers`) are passed through as strings — the API\nnever reshapes the Rule 3 template format."],[784,"**3. Failure isolation (the bug fix).** `bootstrap` now `match`es on `api::spawn`: on\nsuccess it manages the handle and an `ApiInfo::running(port)`; on failure it logs and\nmanages `ApiInfo::disabled()&#x60;. **`app.manage(state)` always runs**, so a missing API never\nbreaks IPC."],[878,"**3. Signing posture is staged.**"],[729,"**3. The desktop reader is deferred.** `tauri-plugin-sql` creates the SQLite file lazily (on\nthe frontend's first `Database.load`), which is *after* the embedded server boots.\n`DeferredReadOnlyStore` opens a fresh read-only connection per request and reports an empty\ndatabase until the file exists, so boot never fails on a missing file. Traffic is local and\nlow-volume, so per-request connection setup is acceptable."],[933,"**3. Updates are signature-verified.** Bundles and the manifest are signed with the\n`tauri-plugin-updater` (minisign) keypair. The **public key is embedded in the app**\n(`tauri.conf.json` updater config); the **private key lives only in CI secrets**\n(`TAURI_SIGNING_PRIVATE_KEY`, `TAURI_SIGNING_PRIVATE_KEY_PASSWORD`) and is never\ncommitted. The plugin refuses any update whose signature does not verify against the\nembedded public key, so transport compromise alone cannot push a malicious update."],[785,"**4. Discovery.** On a successful bind the desktop writes `api-endpoint.json`\n(`{ \"port\", \"base_url\" }`) into the app data dir and exposes an `api_info` IPC command\nreturning `{ running, port, base_url }`. Integrators read either instead of assuming 8080."],[730,"**4. One schema source of truth.** `migrate()` applies the desktop's `migrations/*.sql`,\nembedded by relative path via `include_str!`. It is bootstrap-only (no-op if `templates`\nalready exists), so the server can stand up a fresh database with the exact DDL the desktop\nuses, while schema *evolution* on a shared file stays owned by plugin-sql's migration chain."],[688,"**4. OpenAPI via `utoipa`, served at `/openapi.json`.** Handlers carry `#[utoipa::path]` and\nDTOs derive `ToSchema`, so the spec is generated from the same annotations that wire the\nroutes and cannot drift. `utoipa` is added to core as the single OpenAPI dependency\n(sanctioned by §8.2's `api::router` surface). No Swagger UI is bundled — this is an\noffline-first product; consumers point their own tooling at the JSON."],[881,"**4. The standalone server is a Release asset.** A dedicated build step compiles\n`apps/server` (`shalgalt-server`) for the same targets and uploads the binary (archived\nwith its license/readme) to the *same* Release created by `tauri-action`, so a tag yields\nboth the desktop installer and the headless server in one place."],[934,"**4. UI strings follow the language rule.** The toggle label, the \"update available\" copy,\nand any error surface are **Mongolian**, referenced by key from the P1 string-table — not\nhard-coded. The updater plugin's own `AppError`-style codes stay English (stable\nidentifiers, mapped to Mongolian in the table)."],[27,"**5–10 min**"],[29,"**5–10 min**"],[786,"**5. Developer override.** `SHALGALT_API_PORT` pins the desktop's starting port (fallback\nstill applies). The server keeps `--bind` for explicit control and does **not** fall back."],[689,"**5. Errors reuse the IPC envelope.** `AppError` gains an axum `IntoResponse` that emits the\nexisting `{ code, message }` body with a derived status (`BadRequest` → 400, `NotFound` →\n404, else 500), so REST clients and the Tauri frontend read identical error shapes."],[882,"**5. Native runtime libraries are bundled, not assumed.** Following ADR 0001, the Windows\njob places `opencv_world<NNN>.dll` (and the `pdfium` shared library) where the bundler\nincludes them as Tauri `resources`/`externalBin` so the installed app and the server\nbinary both find their native dependencies on a clean machine."],[731,"**5. SQLite linkage.** `rusqlite`'s `bundled` feature links the same vendored SQLite that\nsqlx (`libsqlite3-sys`) already pulls into the desktop binary; cargo unifies them into one\nSQLite rather than two."],[142,"**5.4 mm**"],[732,"**6. Server assembly is a library.** `apps/server` is split `lib.rs` (CLI + `build_app` +\n`serve`) / `main.rs` so the auth path is integration-testable via `tower::oneshot` without\nbinding a socket. Layer order: auth inside CORS, so the CORS preflight (`OPTIONS`, no\n`Authorization`) is answered before the bearer guard."],[140,"**6.0 mm**"],[146,"**8 pt** Noto Sans Regular"],[751,"**A bind failure bricked the whole app, silently.** `api::spawn(...).await?` propagated\nthe error out of `bootstrap`, which &#x2A;skipped the subsequent `app.manage(state)`*. The\nwindow still opened, but every IPC command that needs `State<AppState>` (scanning, PDF\ngeneration, xlsx export) then failed — with nothing but one line in the log file. A\nsingle port collision turned the app into a silent brick."],[93,"**A. `printpdf` 0.9**"],[304,"**Acceptable**"],[422,"**AMC** (`\\AMCchoiceLabel`), most Mongolian-school cards"],[892,"**Apple Developer account + notarization**: enable the notarization flag and remove the\nskip once credentials exist."],[478,"**ArUco DICT\\_6X6\\_50**"],[665,"**ASCII armor always on** (master plan §6.3). Encrypted entries are written through\n`age::armor::ArmoredWriter` (`Format::AsciiArmor`) and read through `ArmoredReader`. The cost\nis a \\~33 % size increase from base64; the benefit is that ciphertext survives text-oriented\ntransports and is recognizable (`-----BEGIN AGE ENCRYPTED FILE-----`). Because armored\nciphertext is high-entropy base64, encrypted entries are `Stored` (not DEFLATEd) in the zip."],[359,"**B. `Canvas` thin wrapper**"],[420,"**B. Inside the circle**"],[26,"**B. Prebuilt opencv.exe**"],[221,"**Best — runs in workspace test pass**"],[739,"**Blocking I/O on the async executor.** `DataStore` methods are synchronous and run\ndirectly inside the axum handlers, and `SqliteStore` serializes them through one\n`Mutex<Connection>`. For the desktop (single user) and a small-school LAN server (low\nconcurrency, WAL reads) this is acceptable. If concurrency grows, offload the calls with\n`tokio::task::spawn_blocking` (or make `DataStore` async) and/or use a connection pool so\na slow query cannot park a worker thread. Deferred until measured load justifies it."],[182,"**Bounded**"],[631,"**broken** — ZipCrypto is cryptographically weak."],[287,"**Built-in**"],[179,"**Cargo workspace, library crates per concern**"],[781,"**Chosen — `SHALGALT_API_PORT`** for the desktop. It is a developer knob (not user-facing UI), so it does not touch the Mongolian-only UI rule."],[763,"**Chosen — 22345.** In the IANA *registered* range (1024–49151) so the OS never hands it out as an ephemeral port, IANA-unassigned, and nowhere near common dev defaults."],[719,"**Chosen** — `SQLITE_OPEN_READ_ONLY`; cannot write, so plugin-sql stays the only writer."],[724,"**Chosen** — flags for config, `SHALGALT_API_TOKEN` in env (a secret belongs in env, not argv)."],[924,"**Chosen** — free hosting, a stable URL, no infrastructure; matches §6.8's \"static JSON\" model."],[860,"**Chosen** — matches master plan §6.8; the tag is the single version source of truth, and a Release is an explicit, intentional act."],[917,"**Chosen** — no network access until explicitly enabled; offline schools stay silent."],[855,"**Chosen** — official action; creates/updates the Release, builds per-OS bundles, uploads assets, and emits the `latest.json` updater manifest when configured."],[714,"**Chosen** — one impl, both hosts depend on it, core stays clean."],[875,"**Chosen** — one tag, one Release, both products in lockstep."],[870,"**Chosen** — produces an (unnotarized) `.dmg` for testing; flips to notarized once credentials land, no workflow rewrite."],[929,"**Chosen** — the bundle + manifest are signed with the updater keypair; the public key is compiled into the app and verifies every update."],[776,"**Chosen** — the desktop writes `app_data_dir/api-endpoint.json` (`{ port, base_url }`) and exposes an `api_info` command."],[770,"**Chosen** — try `[desired, desired+16)`, bind the first free one, and *isolate the failure* so `app.manage(state)` always runs."],[865,"**Chosen** — unblocks distribution; SmartScreen warns but the artifact is installable and verifiable."],[46,"**CI economy**. Windows GitHub Actions runners cost 2× Linux. A 5–10 min build keeps\nper-commit Windows CI affordable; vcpkg's 30–60 min cold builds would dominate the\npipeline budget and discourage developers from triggering manual builds."],[314,"**Comfort typography (P2-10)**"],[597,"**Compression policy:** small plaintext JSON/CSV is DEFLATE-compressed; entries that do not\nbenefit (already-compressed PDFs, and high-entropy age ciphertext) are `Stored`. flate2's\npure-Rust `zlib-rs` backend is used so there is no system C `zlib` build dependency."],[922,"**Considered** — works, but the endpoint URL changes per tag, awkward to pin."],[683,"**Core is database-free.** `crates/shalgalt-core/CLAUDE.md` forbids any DB on the core\ndependency path; §8.2 pins core's dependencies to `serde`, `thiserror`, `anyhow`,\n`axum`, `tower-http`. The router must reach persistence without core knowing what the\npersistence *is*."],[815,"**Corners + `findHomography` (RANSAC), ≥3 markers**"],[890,"**Coupled to upstream action + URL formats.** `tauri-action` and the OpenCV/pdfium\ndownload URLs are external dependencies; if they break, the workflow needs maintenance."],[344,"**Cursor accumulation in BT/ET.** `Op::SetTextCursor` compiles to PDF `Td` (relative\nmove). Two cursors inside one text section *add*. Bubble labels stacked on the first\nbubble of every group — diagnosed in `bubble_grid.rs`."],[153,"**CV team contract.** P3-01 / P3-02 may assume the values above when designing blob\ndetector parameters and template-fit tolerances. Any future change to the geometry\nrequires a new ADR and a corresponding update to the golden file (regenerated via\n`cargo test -p shalgalt-pdf --test golden -- --ignored regenerate_golden`)."],[368,"**D. One submodule per visual element** (markers, header, bubble\\_grid, labels, manual\\_entry, numeric\\_block, section\\_headers, sidebar)"],[130,"**Deterministic output.** `PdfDocument::new` defaults the `creation_date` /\n`modification_date` to the unix epoch. The trailer's `/ID` array is randomized per\nrun, but is straightforward to strip in golden-file comparisons (see\n`tests/golden.rs::normalize_pdf`). This makes the renderer suitable for the\ngolden-file regression workflow that lands in P2-12."],[974,"**Developer docs (`docs/dev/`) are authored in English** as unsuffixed `.mdx` files. The\nrepository rule \"code & documentation are English only\" forbids a Mongolian translation of\ndeveloper docs, so these pages render in English under **both** locales (the `en` locale\nresolves them directly; the `mn` locale shows the same English content). \"English-first when\ndeveloping\" is therefore satisfied automatically."],[489,"**DICT\\_6X6\\_50** is a sweet spot. The 6×6 inner data grid plus the mandatory\n1-cell black border gives an 8×8 cell footprint. At 200 DPI on A4 portrait this\nprints at roughly 8 × 8 mm — small enough to fit comfortably inside the 12 mm\nouter margin we already reserve for markers, large enough that a 200 DPI raster\nretains 4-5 pixels per cell which is well within `ArucoDetector`'s tolerance."],[1274,"**Excel рүү гаргах** товчоор дүнг `.xlsx` файл болгон татаж авна. Хүснэгтэд сурагчдын оноо,\nасуулт бүрийн үр дүн зэрэг багтана. Баганын гарчиг зэрэг бүх текст **монгол хэл**-ээр гарна."],[984,"**Follow-up**: per-release PDF exports of both manuals (master plan §12) are deferred to\na later docs iteration; the static HTML site is the v1.0 deliverable."],[180,"**Full**"],[962,"**Fumadocs for everything.** A single Next.js documentation framework that covers the\ndeveloper docs, the Mongolian user manual, and the HTTP API reference in one site:"],[156,"**Future ArUco markers.** `MarkerKind::Aruco6x6` is currently rendered as a solid\nsquare placeholder. Bitmap stamping ships with the CV migration in P3-01."],[352,"**How should the layout submodules be organised?**"],[490,"**IDs 0..3** are the smallest non-overlapping identifier set the dictionary\nexposes. Using consecutive IDs makes the slot-to-position mapping trivial:\n`markers[i]` carries ID `i`, and `i ∈ {0, 1, 2, 3}` maps directly to TL/TR/BR/BL."],[346,"**Inside-circle digit position.** Centring math (offset by 30 % of glyph height)\nwas duplicated wherever an in-bubble label was drawn — diagnosed in `bubble_grid.rs`."],[894,"**Installer diet** inherits ADR 0001's P5/P6 follow-up — feature-selective OpenCV build\nif footprint becomes a complaint vector."],[889,"**Installer footprint carries the full native libs** (`opencv_world` \\~70 MB per\nADR 0001, plus `pdfium`). Acceptable for USB / school-network distribution; an installer\ndiet is the ADR 0001 follow-up, not a P6 blocker."],[303,"**Instant**"],[220,"**Light — one dep**"],[893,"**Linux `.deb` + AppImage hardening**: confirm the bundled `pdfium`/OpenCV resolve on\ncommon distros before flipping the Linux job from optional to required (P9)."],[47,"**Local dev parity**. The same five-minute install procedure works on a fresh dev\nmachine. Anything more complicated (vcpkg, custom toolchain) raises the on-ramp for\ncontributors."],[286,"**Low**"],[288,"**Low**"],[880,"**macOS**: notarization is **optional and skipped** when the Apple secrets\n(`APPLE_ID`, `APPLE_PASSWORD`, `APPLE_TEAM_ID`, signing identity) are absent. The build\nstill produces a `.dmg`; it is simply not notarized until an Apple Developer account\nexists."],[960,"**mdBook + VitePress + redoc** (original plan). Three pipelines. mdBook is Rust-native\n(fits the crate docs story) but its theme and search are basic and it has no first-class\nOpenAPI rendering. VitePress covers the user manual well but is a second toolchain."],[956,"**mdBook** for the developer documentation under `docs/dev/`."],[181,"**Medium one-off**"],[98,"**MIT/Apache**"],[155,"**Mongolian script.** `NotoSansMongolian-Regular.ttf` is embedded only when the\ncaller passes `instructions: Some(_)`. Vertical script rendering uses\n`Op::SetTextMatrix(TextMatrix::TranslateRotate(x, y, 90.0))` — `printpdf`'s `Tm`\noperator path. Real shaping (joining contextual forms) is out of scope; if the\nMongolian sidebar reads poorly in production we revisit with `harfbuzz_rs`."],[940,"**Most users stay on whatever they installed.** Because the default is OFF, the\npopulation skews toward old versions; security/bug fixes propagate only to opt-ins or via\nre-download. Acceptable given the offline-first, no-telemetry priorities — manual\nre-install from the Release page is always available."],[1031,"**Multiple credible options** exist and the trade-offs are non-obvious."],[846,"**Native runtime libraries do not statically link.** Per ADR 0001 the Windows build\nlinks `opencv_world<NNN>.dll`, and the CV pipeline also depends on a `pdfium` shared\nlibrary. Both must travel *with* the installer or the produced binary will not launch\non a clean machine."],[193,"**Negative**"],[234,"**Negative**"],[260,"**Negative**"],[325,"**Negative**"],[983,"**Negative**: introduces a Next.js/React dependency tree for docs only. Mitigated by\nisolating it in `docs/site/` (separate install) so it never touches the app build."],[594,"**No custom magic header.** zip's own `PK\\x03\\x04` signature plus the `.shalgalt` extension\nis sufficient to recognize the format. A custom prefix would break \"opens in any zip viewer.\""],[456,"**No error detection.** A scribble in a corner of the page can easily be mistaken\nfor the marker. We get no help from the marker geometry."],[619,"**No hand-rolled crypto.** Repo security policy: never invent crypto; use a vetted library."],[132,"**No higher-level layout engine.** The renderer is data-driven from `OmrTemplate`,\nwhich already encodes every coordinate. A library that re-imposes its own flow\nlayout (genpdf, weasyprint) just gets in the way."],[455,"**No identity.** Four indistinguishable squares cannot tell the CV pipeline which\none is \"top-left.\" If a teacher feeds a sheet upside down or sideways into a\nscanner, the pipeline either guesses the orientation (brittle) or refuses the\npage (annoying for the teacher)."],[907,"**No phone-home.** A version check is itself a network beacon (timestamp, IP, app\nversion). §6.10 rules out anything that smells like telemetry, so a check must only\nhappen when the user has explicitly asked for it."],[845,"**No signing certificates are available yet.** A real Windows EV/OV certificate and an\nApple Developer account are both pending. We cannot block the first tagged build on\nprocurement."],[936,"**No telemetry / no phone-home** by default, satisfying §6.10. A version check only\nexists as a user-initiated action."],[1092,"**Node** ≥ 20 and **pnpm** (the repo pins versions via `package.json` / `pnpm-lock.yaml`)."],[302,"**None — body font-size cascades**"],[97,"**None**"],[750,"**Not configurable.** The port was a hard-coded literal; a teacher had no way to move it."],[906,"**Offline schools must never see an update prompt.** A spinner or \"update available\"\ndialog on a machine with no internet is a support burden and erodes trust in an\noffline-first tool."],[935,"**Offline schools never see a prompt.** Default-OFF means no network access until a\nteacher chooses it — the strongest possible answer to the offline-first requirement."],[85,"**OMR bubble geometry** — physical diameter, stroke width, and pitch of every printed\nbubble. The CV pipeline (P3-01 / P3-02) hard-codes these into its blob detector and\nadaptive threshold parameters, so a renderer that chooses a different value would\nsilently degrade grading accuracy."],[48,"**OpenCV usage is module-light**. shalgalt-omr only calls `core`, `imgproc`,\n`imgcodecs`, and `calib3d`. The 70 MB cost of `opencv_world` is real but bounded —\nit is the difference between a 80 MB and a 20 MB installer, both of which are\nacceptable for USB / school-network distribution at P0–P4. We are not optimising\nfor App Store size limits."],[916,"**Opt-in, default OFF**; teacher flips a toggle"],[71,"**P5 — installer diet**. Re-evaluate vcpkg with feature-selective build if Windows\ninstaller footprint becomes a complaint vector. Target: \\< 30 MB total."],[740,"**Pagination.** List endpoints are currently unbounded (see ADR 0013 — the desktop\nbrowser paginates client-side). A server-enforced page-size cap on `/v1/results` lands\nwhen result volumes warrant it."],[664,"**Passphrase handling:** the passphrase is carried as `age::secrecy::SecretString` (the\nzeroizing wrapper age re-exports — we do not add a separate `secrecy` dependency, keeping the\nversion in lockstep). Encryption uses `age::Encryptor::with_user_passphrase`; decryption uses\n`age::scrypt::Identity`."],[671,"**Passphrase recovery is impossible by design.** There is no escrow, no recovery key, no\nbackdoor. A lost passphrase means a lost project. The optional `manifest.hint` field exists to\nreduce that risk; it MUST NOT contain the passphrase itself. The desktop UI (P4-03/04) must\nwarn the teacher of this at the point they set a passphrase."],[615,"**Passphrase, not key files.** Teachers are non-technical (master plan §7, \"design for older\nteachers\"). Asking them to manage X25519 key pairs is a non-starter. A single passphrase they\nchoose and remember is the only usable model."],[84,"**PDF engine choice** — what library writes the PDF bytes, and what determinism /\nfont / dependency budget we commit to."],[1243,"**PDF болгон гаргах** товчоор хэвлэхэд бэлэн хуудас үүсгэнэ."],[72,"**pdfium bundling** is orthogonal and tracked separately (see README \"pdfium\")."],[618,"**Portability.** A project may travel between machines, mail systems, and zip viewers that\nmangle raw binary. The ciphertext should survive text-oriented transports."],[189,"**Positive**"],[231,"**Positive**"],[256,"**Positive**"],[321,"**Positive**"],[982,"**Positive**: one toolchain, one theme, one search, one deploy; first-class OpenAPI;\nAPI spec stays in sync with the router; docs and updater coexist on one branch."],[154,"**printpdf 0.9 → 0.10 migration.** The `Op` enum and `ParsedFont::from_bytes`\nsignatures changed between 0.7 → 0.8 → 0.9. Pin minor versions; revisit with an ADR\nfollow-up before bumping past 0.9.x."],[129,"**Pure Rust, no system deps.** `shalgalt-pdf` builds on every target the workspace\nalready supports without adding the OpenCV-style Windows install dance. This was the\nsingle biggest factor."],[891,"**Real Windows EV/OV certificate**: procure, store as secrets, retire the self-signed\npath. SmartScreen reputation accrues afterwards."],[765,"**Rejected as the default** — integrators want a stable, documentable port; pure ephemeral makes discovery mandatory every run."],[779,"**Rejected for desktop** — it has no CLI; the server already has `--bind`."],[773,"**Rejected** — a LAN server advertises a fixed port to clients; silently moving it would strand them. Hard fail so the operator picks another `--bind`."],[920,"**Rejected** — a server to run/secure, and an always-on endpoint we'd have to keep alive; over-engineered for a static artifact."],[858,"**Rejected** — couples every release-branch merge to a publish; no clean version anchor."],[712,"**Rejected** — DRY; \\~250 lines of SQL drift between two binaries."],[761,"**Rejected** — equally contended."],[722,"**Rejected** — master plan §6.6 specifies `--bind`/`--db`/`--allow-origin`."],[868,"**Rejected** — needs a paid Apple Developer account we do not yet have."],[853,"**Rejected** — re-implements bundling, signing hooks, and Release upload that `tauri-action` already does."],[863,"**Rejected** — stalls the entire P6 milestone on a procurement timeline we do not control."],[768,"**Rejected** — the embedded API is an *optional* integration surface; the app must keep working without it."],[758,"**Rejected** — the risk is real on the exact machines we target (school PCs) and the failure mode is catastrophic."],[927,"**Rejected** — TLS authenticates the host, not the payload; we want payload integrity independent of transport."],[717,"**Rejected** — two writers on one file; breaks the single-writer rule."],[873,"**Rejected** — version drift between desktop and server."],[915,"**Rejected** — violates §6.10 (silent phone-home) and shows prompts on offline machines."],[710,"**Rejected** — violates §8.2's \"core depends only on serde/thiserror/anyhow/axum/tower-http\"."],[49,"**Reversibility**. A → B is hard (you would have to commit to vcpkg manifests, NuGet\nbinary cache, and a different CI shape). B → A is easy: you replace the install\nstep, set the same three env vars to vcpkg paths, drop `opencv_world` for individual\n`opencv_core` / `opencv_imgproc` libs. We can always migrate later when DLL diet\nbecomes a real constraint."],[347,"**Row-label alignment.** Right-aligning a label against a bubble required hand-rolled\nwidth estimation at every call site — diagnosed in `labels.rs`."],[1093,"**Rust** stable (`rustup`), with `cargo fmt` and `clippy` components."],[887,"**Self-signed Windows builds trigger SmartScreen warnings.** Users must click through\n\"More info → Run anyway\" until a real reputation-bearing cert is installed. Documented\nin the user manual (P7)."],[1298,"**shalgalt-omr** reads and grades OMR (fill-the-bubble) answer sheets automatically,\n**fully offline**, on your own computer. As a teacher you can:"],[351,"**Should layout modules emit raw `Op`s or go through a wrapper?**"],[345,"**Sidebar font fallback.** Mongolian Cyrillic instructions rendered as `.notdef`\nboxes when the caller passed `NotoSansMongolian` to a sidebar that should have used\nLatin/Cyrillic Noto Sans — diagnosed in `sidebar.rs` + `lib.rs`."],[943,"**Signing-key backup + rotation runbook**: document where the private key is stored, how\nit is backed up, and the version-bridged rotation procedure (P7 docs)."],[941,"**Signing-key custody is critical.** Losing `TAURI_SIGNING_PRIVATE_KEY` means no machine\ncan verify a new update (every client trusts only the embedded public key); rotating it\nrequires shipping a new app version with the new public key first. Key backup/rotation is\na documented operational follow-up."],[923,"**Static JSON on the `gh-pages` branch**"],[666,"**Streaming (Rule 1):** the write path pipes plaintext → `Encryptor` `StreamWriter` →\n`ArmoredWriter` → the current zip entry, and the read path reverses it, both via `io::copy`.\nNo entry is buffered whole. The age stream is closed inside-out — finish the `StreamWriter`\n(flush the final ciphertext chunk), then finish the `ArmoredWriter` (emit the armor footer);\nskipping the second finish silently truncates the armor."],[617,"**Streaming (Rule 1).** Encrypting a hundred-MB PDF must not buffer it in memory."],[596,"**Streaming is mandatory (Rule 1).** The `shalgalt-fileformat` crate exposes a `write` that\ntakes an iterator of `(name, ReadHandle)` and an `open` that returns a lending iterator\n(`EntryIter::next_entry`) yielding one borrowed `ReadHandle` at a time. Nothing but the\nmanifest is ever held whole in memory. `ReadHandle::from_path` lets a caller feed a\nmulti-hundred-MB `exam.pdf` straight from disk."],[945,"**String-table keys** for the updater UI land with the toggle implementation (P6-04)."],[938,"**Tamper-resistant.** Signature verification against an embedded public key protects the\nupdate path independently of TLS."],[939,"**Teacher control.** Connected users who *want* updates get a clear, deliberate switch."],[616,"**The manifest must stay readable.** Per 0011, the dashboard\npreviews `manifest.json` without a passphrase. So encryption must be **per-entry**, wrapping\nevery file *except* the manifest — not a whole-archive blob."],[309,"**Theme (P2-09)**"],[491,"**TL/TR/BR/BL** ordering matches the existing `OmrTemplate.markers: [Marker; 4]`\nconvention from `crates/shalgalt-core/src/domain/template.rs`. The grading\nengine, the printpdf renderer, and the perspective-warp routine all consume the\narray in this order."],[131,"**TTF embedding + auto-subsetting.** `PdfSaveOptions { subset_fonts: true, .. }`\nkeeps the output reasonable when only a small Cyrillic / Mongolian-script glyph set\nis actually drawn."],[684,"**Two hosts, one contract.** The desktop and the server must return byte-identical\nresponses for the same data (master plan §10 P5 acceptance), so the endpoint logic\ncannot be duplicated per host."],[847,"**Two products ship from one tag.** The desktop bundle (NSIS + MSI) and the headless\n`shalgalt-server` binary (ADR 0014) are both v1.0 deliverables and should be published\nfrom the same Release so versions never drift."],[888,"**Unnotarized macOS `.dmg` is Gatekeeper-blocked** by default; testers must\nright-click → Open or clear the quarantine attribute. Resolved when notarization is\nswitched on."],[908,"**Updates must still be verifiable.** When a connected teacher *does* opt in, the\nmanifest and bundles must be signature-checked so a tampered update cannot install."],[975,"**User-manual docs (`docs/user/`) are authored in Mongolian** as unsuffixed `.mdx`\n(the `mn` default) with `*.en.mdx` English translations added progressively. Untranslated\nuser pages fall back to Mongolian."],[946,"**Verify default-OFF** is asserted in the v1.0 acceptance checklist (master plan §17 —\n\"Auto-updater is **off** by default\")."],[961,"**VitePress for both surfaces.** One tool, but no first-class OpenAPI rendering (would\nstill need a redoc/embed step) and weaker API-reference ergonomics."],[957,"**VitePress** for the Mongolian user manual under `docs/user/`."],[663,"**What is encrypted:** every zip entry *except* `manifest.json`. `manifest.encrypted = true`\nrecords the choice so a reader knows a passphrase is required for content (but not for the\npreview)."],[879,"**Windows**: a **self-signed certificate** is used for the initial `v0`-class builds.\nThe signing cert/password are read from repository secrets\n(`WINDOWS_CERTIFICATE`, `WINDOWS_CERTIFICATE_PASSWORD`). Swapping to a real EV/OV cert\nis a secret-value change only — the workflow does not change."],[219,"**Wire-format parity**"],[667,"**Wrong-passphrase is a typed error.** A wrong passphrase fails at decrypt time. age 0.11\nsurfaces this as `DecryptError::DecryptionFailed` for a scrypt file (verified against the\ncrate — *not* `NoMatchingKeys`, which the initial research assumed; with scrypt the passphrase\nis the only key, so an unwrap failure is a decryption failure). The crate maps\n`DecryptionFailed`, `KeyDecryptionFailed`, and `NoMatchingKeys` all to a single fieldless\n`FileFormatError::BadPassphrase` (stable code `fileformat.bad_passphrase`). The variant is\nfieldless on purpose so a wrong-passphrase attempt cannot leak age internals into a log or a\nuser-facing message."],[642,"**yes — ASCII armor**"],[582,"**yes — central directory + a named `manifest.json`**"],[641,"**yes — chunked `StreamWriter`/`StreamReader`**"],[584,"**yes — every OS has a zip viewer**"],[583,"**yes — per-entry `Read`/`Write`**"],[96,"**Yes (epoch default)**"],[94,"**Yes**"],[95,"**Yes**"],[937,"**Zero update infrastructure.** A static `gh-pages` file is free, has a stable URL, and\ncannot \"go down\" the way a custom update server can."],[580,"**zip archive**"],[1402,"**Баримт (document) горим**-оор авч, **PDF** хэлбэрээр экспортлоно."],[1241,"**Бөмбөлгийн бүлэг нэмэх** товчоор асуултуудаа нэмж, тоо, зай, оноог тохируулна."],[1237,"**Бөмбөлгийн бүлэг** — асуулт бүрийн сонголтын бөмбөлгүүд (жишээ нь A, B, C, D)."],[1236,"**Булангийн тэмдэг (ArUco)** — хуудасны 4 буланд байрлах бөгөөд програм хуудсыг танихад\nашиглана. Анхдагчаар бэлэн ирнэ."],[1286,"**Дүгнэх** цэс рүү орно."],[1269,"**Дүн** цэс нь хийгдсэн дүгнэлтүүдийн түүхийг харуулна. Та **загвар**-аар, мөн **шалгах\nшаардлагатай** эсэхээр шүүж, хуудаслан үзэж болно. Мөр бүр загвар, огноо, хуудасны тоо,\nшалгах шаардлагатай хуудасны тоог харуулна."],[1398,"**Жигд гэрэлтүүлэг.** Сүүдэр, гялбаа (туссан гэрэл) үүсгэхгүй байх. Цонхны гэрэл налуу\nтусвал сүүдэр үүсдэг."],[1417,"**Нууц үг** зөв эсэхийг шалга. Файлын ерөнхий мэдээллийн **hint** (сануулга) -ыг харж болно."],[1340,"**Нэмэлтээр нууцлах боломжтой** — нууц үг (passphrase) тавьж бүх агуулгыг шифрлэнэ."],[1343,"**Нээх** — `.shalgalt` файл дээр давхар товшиход програм нээгдэж, шифрлэсэн бол нууц үг\nасууна. Агуулга нь таны локал санд сэргээгдэнэ."],[1329,"**Сурагчдын мэдээлэл зөвхөн локал** өгөгдлийн санд хадгалагдана."],[1238,"**Сурагчийн дугаар** ба **Хувилбар**-ын бүлэг (заавал биш)."],[1320,"**Тод хар бэх, хангалттай тонер.** Бүдэг хэвлэлт булангийн ArUco